from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('13');add=A.add
add('3.2.1-01','Absolute zero is 0 K, approximately −273 °C in the school conversion.','The kelvin scale starts at absolute zero, with an interval equal in size to one Celsius degree.','Absolute zero is the lower limit of thermodynamic temperature. In the simple particle account, it corresponds to the limiting minimum thermal motion; do not claim that every form of microscopic motion can be removed. Write kelvin as K without a degree sign. A temperature of 0 °C is the freezing region of water at ordinary pressure, not absolute zero.','Define absolute zero.','Absolute zero is the lower limit of thermodynamic temperature, 0 K.')
add('3.2.1-02','The school conversion is T/K = temperature/°C + 273.','For the reverse conversion, subtract 273 from the kelvin value.','A temperature of 35 °C corresponds to 308 K using this approximation. An interval of 5 °C is an interval of 5 K because the scale steps have the same size; do not add 273 to a temperature difference. Distinguish a point on a scale from a rise between two points.','Calculate the kelvin temperature corresponding to −15 °C using the school conversion.','T = −15 + 273.~The temperature is 258 K.')
add('3.2.1-03','A thermometer uses a measurable property that changes reproducibly with temperature.','Calibration connects property values to known temperatures.','Liquid volume and electrical resistance are thermometric properties. Measure the property at known fixed points or against a calibrated reference, then construct the scale. An approximately linear property permits interpolation using the fraction of the calibration interval. If the response is nonlinear, equal property intervals cannot be assumed to mean equal temperature intervals.','Explain how a changing physical property can become a thermometer.','Link reproducible property changes to known temperature values through calibration.~The calibrated relation then allows an unknown temperature to be inferred.')
add('3.2.1-03-M01','A liquid-in-glass thermometer uses liquid expansion relative to its glass container.','A narrow capillary turns a small volume change into a visible length change.','The liquid is held in a bulb connected to a fine tube. A temperature rise commonly increases liquid volume more than bulb volume, so the column rises. The scale must be calibrated, not simply marked with equal values without checking the response. Wait for thermal equilibrium before reading.','Explain why a liquid thermometer’s column rises when its bulb is warmed.','The liquid expands relative to the containing glass.~The extra volume extends farther along the narrow tube.')
add('3.2.1-03-M02','The electrical resistance of a suitable metal changes with temperature.','A resistance thermometer infers temperature from a calibrated resistance measurement.','A small sensing element is brought into thermal contact with the sample. A measuring circuit records resistance, which is compared with its calibration. Over an appropriate limited range, a metal resistance can be approximated as increasing linearly. Avoid excessive measurement current because self-heating changes the sensor temperature.','Explain how a calibrated metal resistor measures temperature.','Its resistance changes reproducibly as its temperature changes.~The measured resistance is matched to a calibrated temperature value.')
add('3.2.1-03-M03','For a linear response, the fraction of a property’s calibration interval equals the fraction of its temperature interval.','Between 0 and 100 °C, θ = 100(X − X0)/(X100 − X0).','Subtract the lower-point value before taking the ratio. A column of 15 mm at 0 °C and 115 mm at 100 °C gives 40 °C when its length is 55 mm. The full length is not proportional to Celsius temperature unless the lower-point reading is zero. Interpolate within the calibrated range; extrapolation requires further evidence.','Calculate temperature for a linear thermometer reading 50 mm when its readings are 10 mm at 0 °C and 90 mm at 100 °C.','θ = 100(50 − 10)/(90 − 10).~The temperature is 50 °C.')
add('3.2.1-04','Sensitivity, range and linearity describe different thermometer properties.','A sensitive instrument need not have a wide range or a linear response.','Sensitivity is output change per temperature change, range is the usable temperature interval, and linearity describes whether equal temperature changes give equal output changes. Choose according to the task: a narrow clinical interval and a furnace measurement impose different needs. Response time is another property, distinct from sensitivity.','Explain why sensitivity and range must both be considered when selecting a thermometer.','Link sensitivity to the output change available for small temperature differences.~Range determines whether the required temperatures can be measured at all.')
add('3.2.1-04-M01','Sensitivity is the change in thermometric property per unit temperature change.','For the same bulb and liquid, a narrower capillary increases column movement per degree.','A column that moves 2 mm per degree is more sensitive than one moving 0.5 mm per degree. This can make small changes easier to distinguish, but accuracy also depends on calibration, reading and thermal contact. A very fine scale does not prove an accurate thermometer.','Explain how sensitivity and range differ.','Sensitivity gives property change per degree.~Range gives the interval between the lowest and highest usable temperatures.')
add('3.2.1-04-M02','Thermometer range is the interval between its usable lower and upper temperatures.','It differs from sensitivity, which concerns response per degree.','A liquid’s freezing and boiling behaviour, the tube length and the material’s durability constrain usable range. A high sensitivity on a fixed-length stem can limit how large a temperature interval fits. State the actual endpoints when comparing instruments rather than calling one range simply better.','Explain how thermometer range differs from sensitivity.','Range gives the usable temperature interval.~Sensitivity gives the property change for each temperature degree within an appropriate range.')
add('3.2.1-04-M03','A linear thermometer gives equal output increments for equal temperature increments.','A nonlinear thermometer can still be calibrated, but requires an appropriate nonuniform scale or relation.','A straight calibration graph supports a constant sensitivity over the tested range. A curved graph has varying slope, so one constant mm-per-degree value does not apply throughout. A straight response does not establish that the zero mark is correct; linearity and calibration offset are different issues.','Explain the distinction between linear and nonlinear calibration responses.','A linear response has equal output changes for equal temperature changes.~A nonlinear response has a sensitivity that varies across its range.')
add('3.2.1-05','Fixed points provide reproducible temperature references for calibration.','The ice point and steam point of pure water at specified standard pressure are approximately 0 °C and 100 °C.','Pressure and purity matter for these references. Use a well-mixed ice-water equilibrium for the lower point rather than a random ice cube surface. The upper reference uses steam in the specified equilibrium conditions, not an arbitrary hot vessel. A comparison against a calibrated reference thermometer can establish additional points safely in moderate baths.','State the purpose of thermometer fixed points.','They provide reproducible known temperatures for calibrating the scale.')
add('3.2.1-06','A liquid-in-glass thermometer has a bulb, liquid and narrow capillary with a scale.','A larger bulb or narrower bore generally increases column movement per degree, with other features fixed.','For a fixed stem length, greater sensitivity usually reduces the temperature interval that fits. A wider bore can offer greater range but smaller movement per degree. A uniform bore helps linearity when the liquid response is approximately uniform. Good thermal contact and sufficient waiting time are needed before reading the meniscus squarely.','Explain why narrowing a thermometer capillary increases its sensitivity with the same bulb.','The same liquid volume increase occupies a greater tube length.~The column therefore moves farther per degree.')
add('3.2.1-07','Thermometric liquids differ in expansion, visibility, usable temperature range and hazards.','An alcohol-based liquid can serve lower temperatures than mercury, while its lower boiling range limits high-temperature use.','Mercury is visible and does not wet clean glass strongly, but it is toxic and should not be handled in student experiments. Coloured alcohol is easier to see than a clear liquid and can have a large expansion response, but may wet glass. Material choice must match the intended interval rather than assuming one liquid is best for every measurement.','Explain one range difference between alcohol and mercury as thermometric liquids.','Alcohol can remain liquid at lower temperatures than mercury.~Its lower boiling point limits high-temperature use compared with mercury.')
add('3.2.1-08','A thermocouple uses two different conductors joined at a sensing junction and a reference junction or equivalent compensated reference.','A temperature difference produces a small electrical output that can be calibrated.','A small junction has low thermal capacity and can respond rapidly. Appropriate metals permit high-temperature measurements beyond many liquid-in-glass instruments. The signal depends on the junction temperatures and requires calibration; it is not simply a resistance thermometer. Keep the measuring arrangement suitable for the temperature and environment.','Describe how a thermocouple can measure a rapidly changing high temperature.','A small junction of two different conductors develops a calibrated electrical output relative to a reference, and its low thermal capacity allows rapid response.')
A.misconception('MIS-28','Zero degrees Celsius is not absolute zero.','The school conversion places 0 °C at 273 K and absolute zero at 0 K.','Both scales use equal interval sizes, but their origins differ. A claim that ice water has no thermal motion because its temperature is zero confuses a scale label with the thermodynamic lower limit. Use the conversion on the temperature point rather than treating a zero Celsius reading as zero energy.','A learner says water at 0 °C is at absolute zero. Explain the confusion.','The learner has confused the origins of the Celsius and kelvin scales.~0 °C is about 273 K; absolute zero is 0 K, about −273 °C.')
C=A.choice
C('3.2.1-01','Which temperature is absolute zero?',('0 K','Kelvin starts at this lower limit.'),('0 °C','This is approximately 273 K.'),('273 °C','This is well above absolute zero.'),('100 K','This is above 0 K.'))
C('3.2.1-03-M01','Which property is used in a liquid-in-glass thermometer?',('Liquid expansion','It changes column length.'),('Liquid mass increasing with heat','No mass addition is needed.'),('Its fixed colour alone','A static colour is not this measurement property.'),('The room’s weight','That is not the sensing property.'))
C('3.2.1-04-M01','What describes thermometer sensitivity?',('Output change per degree','This is response per temperature change.'),('Lowest temperature alone','This is one range limit.'),('The largest possible temperature','This is an upper limit.'),('Time until equilibrium','This is response time.'))
C('3.2.1-04-M03','Which calibration graph indicates linear response?',('A straight line','It has constant slope.'),('A changing-slope curve','This is nonlinear.'),('Unrelated scattered points','That does not establish a reproducible relation.'),('A vertical line across many temperatures','Temperature belongs on an independent calibration axis.'))
C('3.2.1-05','Why are fixed points used?',('To establish known temperature references','They support calibration.'),('To remove the need for units','Units remain necessary.'),('To make every liquid equally suitable','Material properties still differ.'),('To prevent thermal equilibrium','Equilibrium is desirable.'))
C('3.2.1-08','Why can a small thermocouple respond rapidly?',('Its junction has low thermal capacity','Less energy is needed for its temperature to change.'),('It contains a large liquid tank','That is not its construction.'),('It needs no calibration','Its signal still needs calibration.'),('It measures mass directly','Its output relates to junction temperatures.'))
C('3.2.1-07','Which issue makes mercury inappropriate for casual student handling?',('Toxicity','Hazard is a material-selection concern.'),('It is a gas at room temperature','It is liquid under ordinary room conditions.'),('It cannot expand','It does expand.'),('It has no visible surface','It is readily visible.'))
C('3.2.1-02','Using the school conversion, what is 27 °C?',('300 K','Add 273.'),('246 K','This subtracts from 273.'),('27 K','This ignores the scale offset.'),('327 K','This adds an incorrect offset.'))
C('3.2.1-02','A temperature rises by 8 °C. What is the rise in kelvin?',('8 K','The interval sizes are equal.'),('281 K','Do not add the offset to a difference.'),('265 K','Do not subtract an offset from a difference.'),('0 K','The temperature has changed.'))
C('3.2.1-03-M03','A linear sensor reads 20 at 0 °C and 120 at 100 °C. What is a reading of 45?',('25 °C','It is 25 units above the lower point.'),('45 °C','This ignores the offset.'),('65 °C','This adds the offset instead of subtracting.'),('75 °C','This uses the remaining interval.'))
C('3.2.1-06','A capillary bore narrows with the bulb unchanged. What is expected?',('Greater column rise per degree','The same extra volume occupies more length.'),('Smaller rise per degree','This reverses the geometry effect.'),('More liquid mass created','Heating does not add material.'),('No effect on movement','Bore area affects length.'))
C('3.2.1-04-M01','Sensor X changes 12 mm over 4 °C. What is its sensitivity?',('3 mm/°C','Divide output change by temperature change.'),('48 mm/°C','This multiplies them.'),('0.333 °C/mm','This is the reciprocal.'),('8 mm/°C','This subtracts unlike quantities.'))
P=A.part
P('3.2.1-01','State the kelvin temperature at absolute zero.','0 K.',1,'One mark for 0 K.')
P('3.2.1-05','State one fixed-point temperature for pure water at standard pressure.','Ice point, 0 °C.',1,'One mark for an appropriate named point and value.')
P('3.2.1-06','Explain the effect of a narrower capillary on sensitivity.','The same liquid expansion occupies more length. The column therefore moves farther for each degree.',2,'One mark for volume-length reasoning; one for greater response per degree.')
P('3.2.1-02','Calculate the kelvin temperature of 42 °C using +273.','T = 42 + 273 = 315 K.',2,'One mark for the conversion; one for 315 K.')
P('3.2.1-03-M03','Calculate temperature for a linear column reading 70 mm, calibrated at 10 mm for 0 °C and 110 mm for 100 °C.','θ = 100(70 − 10)/(110 − 10) = 60 °C.',2,'One mark for offset-corrected fraction; one for 60 °C.')
A.practical=dict(graph='Plot liquid-column length vertically against reference temperature horizontally; use a calibration line rather than forcing it through zero.',unit='mm/°C',meaning='The gradient is the thermometer’s length sensitivity. A nonzero length at 0 °C is a calibration offset, not evidence of a wrong slope.',method=['Place the uncalibrated liquid thermometer and a calibrated reference in the same stirred water bath.','Set a moderate bath temperature and wait for stable readings.','Record reference temperature and uncalibrated column length without removing the sensors.','Repeat over several temperatures within the instrument’s safe range.','Repeat while cooling and compare readings for consistency.'],control='Keep immersion depth and reading geometry fixed, avoid contact with the vessel wall and handle warm water safely.',table='Record reference temperature / °C and liquid-column length / mm, with repeat readings.',conclusion='Use the fitted calibration relation to infer unknown temperatures within the tested range and its gradient to quantify sensitivity.')
if __name__=='__main__':A.build()
